    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Frontier models & labs",
      "headline": "Anthropic reports agent memory lifts Opus 5.5 database-task reward from 0.655 to 0.838, and wiki building from 13% to 48%",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "Anthropic published an engineering post on how agent memory actually works, and it comes with numbers. On a database task, Opus 5.5 went from a mean reward of 0.655 with no memory to 0.838 with a memory store. Opus 5 went from 0.564 to 0.828, which is close to what the newest model manages." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "There's a second experiment I found more interesting. Anthropic let the model read a document corpus of about 350,000 files offline, over and over, and build itself a wiki. After 2 of those sessions the wiki answered 13% of the graded criteria. After 128 sessions, 48%." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "So repetition on the same documents keeps paying off." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "It does, but the caveats matter. These are its own numbers, on its own internal benchmark and its own corpus, and they are not independently verified. Anthropic is a single source for all of it, and the post doesn't report what the repeated sessions cost to run." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "To the research, where a weekend of papers landed all at once." }
      ]
    },
