      "section": "Security, misuse & threat intelligence",
      "headline": "Microsoft says Cambodia-based Storm-3358 began using the ARTEX agent framework against financial firms in October 2026",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "Microsoft says that in October 2026 a financially motivated threat actor it tracks began targeting organisations in the financial sector with the agentic attack framework we've been following. Microsoft describes the group as a Cambodia-based intrusion cluster that mainly goes after financial organisations in Southeast Asia." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "What does the attack actually look like?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Microsoft says the agents scanned internet-facing systems, exploited flaws including anonymous file upload and server-side request forgery, then used netcat and curl to pull down remote-access trojans for persistence and lateral movement." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "There's a defensive finding in here too, and I thought it was the most useful part. Microsoft says honeypots, decoys, tarpits and what it calls fake wins, which is convincing evidence that makes an agent stop on a false success, helped reduce the attack traffic reaching real assets in its own testing." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Although Microsoft publishes no numerical measurement of that effect, so we don't know how big the benefit is. This is one company's attribution and telemetry, and it is not independently verified." }
      ]
    },
