    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Security, misuse & threat intelligence",
      "headline": "Apiiro finds 1,122 public copies of Claude Code's permission file holding 1,379 secrets, 2,539 counting history",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "This is the one every developer should hear. The Hacker News reports that the security firm Apiiro searched GitHub and found 104,185 copies of a Claude Code settings file. Of those, 1,122 held at least one secret, and together they held 1,379 different secrets." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "How does a secret end up in a settings file?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "When you click yes, and don't ask again, the tool can save the approved command exactly as you typed it, including a secret typed inline. More than a third of the secrets open a database directly, including 379 database passwords, plus 43 SSH logins for public servers, 42 tokens for GitHub, GitLab and npm, and 23 AWS keys." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "And how long have they been sitting there?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "In half of the repositories with a secret, the file had been on the default branch for at least 217 days. Counting older versions, forks and saved chat logs, the total comes to 2,539 secrets across 1,994 repositories. These are its own numbers, from a single source, its write-up could not be read directly, and Apiiro did not test whether the keys still work, though The Hacker News says one notified company confirmed its key had been live. Anthropic's response is not recorded in the article." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Security, misuse & threat intelligence",
      "headline": "Google DeepMind says AI agents cut an RSA-1024 signature forgery to 391 CPU core-years from 1,380",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "SecurityWeek reports that Google DeepMind, with cryptographers from UC San Diego and Inria, says AI agents under human supervision found two ways to reduce the cost of attacking 1024-bit RSA. In a sandboxed run, the first signature forgery took 391 CPU core-years, against 1,380 in the original study." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And how much of that did the agents do themselves?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "SecurityWeek says two agents designed, ran and debugged the computation in under four days, with roughly 20 messages from the human researcher, mostly approvals." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "The limits are substantial. The key was never factored. SecurityWeek says that would take an estimated 500,000 to 1 million core-years. The attack needs roughly 4 billion signing queries made in advance, does not recover the private key, and does not work against devices that hash messages internally." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "And the two bigger-sounding figures are theoretical estimates. Against a 32-byte hash, DeepMind reports an algorithm roughly 1,600 times better than the previous best method against 1024-bit keys. A second finding would make each additional 1024-bit key roughly 30 times cheaper on average than the best previous approach, but SecurityWeek says that method has not been implemented and its estimate assumes an upfront computation as costly as factoring one key." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Pushmeet Kohli of Google DeepMind put it directly, saying these results only affect obsolete 1024-bit systems, and that modern protocols, which almost universally use 2048-bit or larger keys, remain secure. These are its own numbers, SecurityWeek is the only source we could open, and none of it has been independently reproduced." }
      ]
    },
