      "type": "intro",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "It's Monday, November 9th, and this is The AI Edge, presented by Epilogue." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Epilogue is an AI venture studio and consultancy in Toronto that builds for high-consequence work, the kind that's document-dense, driven by precedent, and reviewed by people whose licence is on the line. Epilogue builds products where the answer has to be right. Find out more at epiloguelabs.com." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "I'm Maya." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And I'm Alex." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Here's what moved at the frontier of AI since Friday morning. The advances, the research, and how the technology is being used for good and for harm, with every claim traced back to the source it came from." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "It's a long weekend's worth of news. What are the three that matter most?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Nvidia's board said on November 8th that it is opening an independent investigation after reports that its chief executive, Jensen Huang, had been the target of a foreign intelligence effort. Huang told staff the same day that he had been unfaithful to his wife." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Microsoft says a financially motivated criminal group began using an agentic attack framework against the financial sector in October 2026, and a security firm found 1,122 public copies of a Claude Code settings file holding 1,379 secrets." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "And Google DeepMind says AI agents under human supervision cut the cost of forging an RSA-1024 signature down to 391 CPU core-years, from 1,380 in the original study." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "Let's start with the labs, and with what happens when you give a model somewhere to keep what it learns." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Frontier models & labs",
      "headline": "Anthropic reports agent memory lifts Opus 5.5 database-task reward from 0.655 to 0.838, and wiki building from 13% to 48%",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "Anthropic published an engineering post on how agent memory works, and it comes with numbers. On a database task, Opus 5.5 went from a mean reward of 0.655 with no memory to 0.838 with a memory store. Opus 5 went from 0.564 to 0.828." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "There's a second experiment I found more interesting. Anthropic let the model read a corpus of about 350,000 files offline, repeatedly, and build itself a wiki. After 2 of those sessions the wiki answered 13% of the graded criteria. After 128 sessions, 48%." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "And what should we hold back on?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Quite a lot. These are its own numbers, on its own internal benchmark and its own corpus, and they are not independently verified. Anthropic is a single source for all of it. The post doesn't report variance across runs, or what the sessions cost to run, and Sonnet 4.6 barely improved, going from 0.330 to 0.364, which Anthropic puts down to prompting." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "To the research, where a weekend of papers landed all at once." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Research & papers",
      "headline": "Google DeepMind agents write Lean-verified quantum programs, reporting a 290x gain on quantum arithmetic",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "A paper from Google DeepMind and Google Quantum AI researchers put language model agents to work writing quantum programs inside the Lean theorem prover, where the programs the agents write are machine-checked for correctness and for cost." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And they improved on what people had already built?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "On arXiv they report gains over prior baselines of up to 1.4x on parallel search, 290x on quantum arithmetic, and on simulations relevant to fusion, photochemistry and drug metabolism, 11x, 8x and 2.5x." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "The formal verification is what makes this unusual. Most agent results you have to take on trust. These ones are proof-checked." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Though this is a preprint, so it is not peer reviewed, and arXiv is the only source for it. The arXiv page shows no affiliations, there's no accompanying DeepMind blog post, and the speedups are measured against baselines the authors chose themselves." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "Now to security, which took up most of this weekend." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Security, misuse & threat intelligence",
      "headline": "Microsoft says Cambodia-based Storm-3358 began using the ARTEX agent framework against financial firms in October 2026",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "Microsoft says that in October 2026 a financially motivated threat actor it calls Storm-3358 began targeting organisations in the financial sector with the agentic attack framework we've been following. Microsoft describes it as a Cambodia-based intrusion cluster that primarily targets financial organisations in Southeast Asia, plus a smaller number of large financial organisations globally." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "What does the attack actually look like?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Microsoft says the group used the framework alongside conventional tooling to scan internet-facing systems and exploit flaws including anonymous file upload and server-side request forgery, then used netcat and curl to download remote-access trojans for persistence and lateral movement." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "There's a defensive finding here too, and I thought it was the most useful part. Microsoft says honeypots, decoys, tarpits and what it calls fake wins, which is convincing evidence that makes an agent stop on a false success, helped reduce the attack traffic reaching real assets in its own testing." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Although Microsoft publishes no numerical measurement of that effect, so we don't know how big the benefit is. This is one company's attribution and telemetry, and it is not independently verified." }
      ]
    },
